# Names asked of the runner's own DNS server (N3; `make test-net-dns`):
# tools/test-dns on the host's 127.0.0.1 answers `localhost` with 127.0.0.1 and
# every other name with NXDOMAIN; the disk's `net.dns = 10.0.2.2` points the
# guest at it. The step runs inside tools/confine-loopback.sh, so no query
# reaches an outside resolver (INV-0230-1). The resolver's logic is also covered
# by `cargo test -p zoraya-net` with a scripted DNS server.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# `localhost` is answered with 127.0.0.1 (no Internet needed): an
# address of this side of the network, so the connection is refused as local
# (ADR-0223 item 4). A name that does not exist (`.invalid`, RFC 2606) is
# unresolvable.
> fetch http://localhost:80/\r
< the name points at this machine's own network
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://nothing.invalid/\r
< the name has no address
< \r\n~>
# `ping` resolves a name inside the network service, under an echo-only grant for
# that name, with the same refusals as `fetch`.
> ping localhost\r
< the name points at this machine's own network
< \r\n~>
> ping nothing.invalid\r
< the name has no address
< \r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
